k43 (commit 900f3e5) implements the Regex class per ADR 0013 but the matching behaviour of the g and y flags diverges from JS:

- Oracle: r/x/y.test("yxy") renders "false" (JS /y is sticky, no match at position 0).
- Perl port: renders "true" (y flag ignored, single match anywhere).

- Oracle: stateful global iteration set r = r/\d/g then m1 = r.exec("1 2 3") twice and m2 and m3 likewise yields "1|2|3" (lastIndex advances across calls).
- Perl port: yields the same value for each call (no lastIndex tracking). With capture-group patterns the array shape breaks because Perl qr// in list context with no groups returns a builtin::true boolean which gets prepended to captures and routed through is_bool in _js_to_string.

ADR 0013 requires:
- y (sticky) to map to \G-anchored matching (the port ignores y entirely).
- g to be carried on the object and honoured by test/replace/global matching (not a qr// flag).

make_regex records the flags on the object but test() and exec() use a stateless qr// ($str =~ $self->_qr). Worker comment acknowledges "out of scope for a stateless qr// ... grow on demand" and "oracle-confirmed". The oracle-confirmed claim holds for the 13 corpus cases added in k43 (one-shot test/exec). It does not hold for stateful or sticky cases, which are observable in templates today.

Per ADR 0004 (a difference without an ADR is a bug), ADR 0013 already documents the intended behaviour but the implementation does not honour it.

Required fix: track lastIndex on the Regex object as a rw Moo attribute initialised to 0; reset to 0 on test/exec when g flag absent; honour \G anchor when y flag set; advance lastIndex after a successful match when g or y. Add corpus cases that exercise stateful iteration (g) and sticky matching (y) to pin the contract.

Re-claiming: fix lastIndex tracking + sticky /\G anchor. Dispatched after k43 audit. Will run before #48 because both touch Regex::exec.

